> For both of these, it is clearly useful to deal with basic array types, and perhaps less so for more complex (non-sequential or R-idiosyncratic) objects.
>
> Or are you thinking of
> (iii) creating a full-fledged alternative to base:save, base:load?

Along the lines of (iii), to get the benefits of (i) and (ii). For instance, 
'transparently' reading in a slice of a GRanges object (or more simply a factor 
or data.frame), instead of needing to write code to martial the individual 
components.

I agree that truly complicated structures might be too difficult to use outside 
R, but a factor, data.frame, etc would be parse-able. Some of the 'sugar' in 
Rcpp shows this.

